Because the loss did not only remove value. It produced information. It exposed contractor risk, legal-release risk, tenant risk, cost-overrun risk, and plan risk all at once. If $K_t$ is operating knowledge at stage $t$, and $L_t$ is the lesson extracted from a painful project, the update is $K_{t+1}=K_t+L_t.$ The danger is to write only $\text{project loss}=\text{failure}.$ The interview’s actual logic is closer to $\text{project loss}
+
\text{survival}
+
\text{lesson extraction}
=
\text{future capability}.$

